    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. regarding the mTOR part, how exactly does the cell \"sense\" the leucine levels? is there a specific sensor protein involved in that mechanism?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The mTOR pathway responds to changes in leucine levels through a key sensor protein called Rag1 (renal gag homolog 1). Rag1 is part of the inhibitory pathway of mTOR, and it recognizes leucine-bound aminoacyl-tRNA, which is a signal of increased metabolic activity. When Rag1 senses a rise in leucine levels, it releases an inhibitory signal that collects on mTOR, initially inhibiting mTOR activity. When leucine levels drop, the inhibitory feedback decreases and mTOR activity is reactivated. This feedback loop ensures that mTOR is only activated in response to increased leucine availability.",
      "source": "target",
